{
  "unit": "tool_agent_kg__PMC11375712__broad",
  "auditor": "trapi:gpt-5.6-sol_2026-07-09",
  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "The relevant material family is Fe-Co-Ni-derived fcc high-entropy alloys, specifically the CoCrFeMnNi Cantor alloy.",
      "field": "hypothesis",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "partial",
      "supporting_ids": [
        "PMC6093928::chunk::000::abstract::b3f2cb8881",
        "PMC6093928::chunk::001::introduction::2dc33c3a9e"
      ],
      "same_system": "unclear"
    },
    {
      "text": "The targeted property is the understanding and control of finite-temperature magnetic characteristics, particularly magnetic moments.",
      "field": "hypothesis",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "partial",
      "supporting_ids": [
        "PMC6093928::chunk::000::abstract::b3f2cb8881",
        "PMC6093928::chunk::001::introduction::2dc33c3a9e",
        "PMC6093928::chunk::002::results_and_discussion::13b860da4c"
      ],
      "same_system": "yes"
    },
    {
      "text": "Finite-temperature magnetic behavior affects phase stability and stacking-fault energy in CoCrFeMnNi.",
      "field": "hypothesis",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "entailed",
      "supporting_ids": [
        "PMC6093928::chunk::000::abstract::b3f2cb8881",
        "PMC6093928::chunk::003::methods::f3287d9a39"
      ],
      "same_system": "yes"
    },
    {
      "text": "Orbital magnetic moments are often neglected or difficult to isolate in paramagnetic states.",
      "field": "hypothesis",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Longitudinal spin fluctuations induce sizable magnetic moments for Co, Cr, and Ni in paramagnetic CoCrFeMnNi.",
      "field": "hypothesis",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "entailed",
      "supporting_ids": [
        "PMC6093928::chunk::000::abstract::b3f2cb8881",
        "PMC6093928::chunk::002::results_and_discussion::13b860da4c"
      ],
      "same_system": "yes"
    },
    {
      "text": "The orbital contribution to the total magnetic moment and its temperature dependence remain poorly characterized in current state-of-the-art models.",
      "field": "hypothesis",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "CoCrFeMnNi is polymorphic but usually has a chemically disordered fcc structure at ambient pressure.",
      "field": "material_initial_state",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "entailed",
      "supporting_ids": [
        "PMC6093928::chunk::001::introduction::2dc33c3a9e"
      ],
      "same_system": "yes"
    },
    {
      "text": "Fcc CoCrFeMnNi has a paramagnetic regime and low-temperature magnetic transitions.",
      "field": "material_initial_state",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "entailed",
      "supporting_ids": [
        "PMC6093928::chunk::001::introduction::2dc33c3a9e"
      ],
      "same_system": "yes"
    },
    {
      "text": "First-principles alloy theory can account for longitudinal spin fluctuations and thermal lattice expansion when evaluating magnetic structure and phase stability.",
      "field": "intervention",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "entailed",
      "supporting_ids": [
        "PMC6093928::chunk::000::abstract::b3f2cb8881",
        "PMC6093928::chunk::003::methods::f3287d9a39"
      ],
      "same_system": "yes"
    },
    {
      "text": "The modeled temperature range extends from 50 K to 1200 K.",
      "field": "conditions",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "partial",
      "supporting_ids": [
        "PMC6093928::chunk::002::results_and_discussion::13b860da4c"
      ],
      "same_system": "yes"
    },
    {
      "text": "Longitudinal spin fluctuations induce sizable Co, Cr, and Ni moments in both fcc and hcp phases.",
      "field": "structural_mediator",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "entailed",
      "supporting_ids": [
        "PMC6093928::chunk::000::abstract::b3f2cb8881",
        "PMC6093928::chunk::002::results_and_discussion::13b860da4c"
      ],
      "same_system": "yes"
    },
    {
      "text": "In conventional floating-spin calculations, Cr and Ni have zero moments, while Co develops a finite moment only at large volumes.",
      "field": "mechanism",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "entailed",
      "supporting_ids": [
        "PMC6093928::chunk::002::results_and_discussion::13b860da4c"
      ],
      "same_system": "yes"
    },
    {
      "text": "Thermally induced longitudinal spin fluctuations modify phase stability, stacking-fault energy, and fcc-hcp interfacial energy.",
      "field": "mechanism",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "entailed",
      "supporting_ids": [
        "PMC6093928::chunk::000::abstract::b3f2cb8881",
        "PMC6093928::chunk::003::methods::f3287d9a39"
      ],
      "same_system": "yes"
    },
    {
      "text": "The cited computational model does not explicitly incorporate orbital-moment contributions.",
      "field": "mechanism",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "partial",
      "supporting_ids": [
        "PMC6093928::chunk::003::methods::f3287d9a39"
      ],
      "same_system": "yes"
    },
    {
      "text": "When longitudinal spin fluctuations and thermal expansion are included, mean magnetic moments increase with temperature.",
      "field": "predicted_outcome",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "entailed",
      "supporting_ids": [
        "PMC6093928::chunk::002::results_and_discussion::13b860da4c"
      ],
      "same_system": "yes"
    },
    {
      "text": "The hcp phase becomes energetically preferable at cryogenic temperatures, producing negative stacking-fault energy.",
      "field": "predicted_outcome",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "entailed",
      "supporting_ids": [
        "PMC6093928::chunk::000::abstract::b3f2cb8881"
      ],
      "same_system": "yes"
    },
    {
      "text": "Conventional floating-spin calculations produce magnetic moments that depend only on volume and therefore omit temperature-induced longitudinal spin fluctuations.",
      "field": "baseline",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "entailed",
      "supporting_ids": [
        "PMC6093928::chunk::002::results_and_discussion::13b860da4c",
        "PMC6093928::chunk::003::methods::f3287d9a39"
      ],
      "same_system": "yes"
    },
    {
      "text": "Conventional floating-spin calculations neglect orbital-moment contributions.",
      "field": "baseline",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "The conclusions apply to similar Fe-Co-Ni-based fcc high-entropy alloys in paramagnetic regimes where longitudinal spin fluctuations are significant.",
      "field": "boundary",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "partial",
      "supporting_ids": [
        "PMC6093928::chunk::001::introduction::2dc33c3a9e",
        "PMC6093928::chunk::003::methods::f3287d9a39"
      ],
      "same_system": "unclear"
    },
    {
      "text": "Temperature-dependent XMCD can directly quantify orbital moments in CoCrFeMnNi for comparison with longitudinal-spin-fluctuation predictions.",
      "field": "falsification_experiment",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
